Reading of the question: the three types are taken as static electricity, direct current (d.c.) and alternating current (a.c.), and the question asks how each is generated (produced).

1. Static electricity: produced by friction (rubbing two different insulators, so electrons are transferred, e.g. a glass rod and silk), by induction, or by contact. Machines such as the Van de Graaff generator and Wimshurst machine produce large static charges. The charges stay at rest on the surface.

2. Direct current (d.c.): flows in one direction only. It is generated by:
• chemical action in cells and batteries (e.g. the Daniell cell, lead–acid accumulator), which convert chemical energy into electrical energy;
• solar (photovoltaic) cells;
• thermocouples;
• d.c. generators (dynamos with a split-ring commutator);
• rectifying a.c.

3. Alternating current (a.c.): reverses direction periodically. It is generated by a.c. generators (alternators), by electromagnetic induction. A coil rotates in a magnetic field (or a magnet rotates in a coil); the changing flux induces an e.m.f. E = E₀ sin ωt (Faraday's law), collected by slip rings. Power stations (hydro, thermal, gas) use turbines to drive alternators.
